.TH MDNS 8 "October 2026" "mdns 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mdns \- mDNS discovery and reflection daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdns
[\fIconfig-file\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdns
tracks the devices announcing themselves with multicast DNS (Bonjour, Avahi)
on a set of network interfaces, and reflects them between the interfaces you
choose, and only those.
Given
.B br0 <-> media
and
.BR "br0 <-> wifi" ,
the
.B media
and
.B wifi
networks never learn about each other's devices, even though both talk to
.BR br0 .
.PP
For each interface and each address family (IPv4 and IPv6, which are kept
completely separate) the daemon opens one socket, bound to that interface and
to the mDNS multicast group address.
It never listens on the wildcard address.
.PP
What may be reflected where is described by the
.B [Segment]
sections of the configuration file; see
.BR mdnsd.conf (5).
.SS Tracking
A device is known by its source address.
Its hostname and its DNS-SD services are taken from the records it announces.
A device expires after it has been silent for at least five minutes, or for the
TTL of its records if that is longer.
A goodbye packet (TTL 0) removes it immediately.
.SS Probing
Devices announce themselves rarely, and some only answer when asked, so waiting
is not enough.
The daemon therefore also asks.
A probe is three rounds of multicast queries, a few seconds in all: the first
asks which service types exist; the next browses every type learned so far for
instances; and instances that have not yet said where they are (their target
and port) are resolved by asking for their service and text records.
A device needs a hostname and a service to be reflected, and the resolve step is
what supplies the hostname for devices that only answer browse queries.
.PP
A probe runs on every interface once at startup, and again whenever
.B mdnsctl probe
is run.
Probes are not repeated on a timer.
.SS Advertising
With
.B Hostname=
in the
.B [Advertise]
section of
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
the daemon also announces and answers for its own name, for example
.BR router.local ,
the way Avahi does for the host it runs on.
On each interface it publishes the address of this host on that interface (an
A record with the primary IPv4 address, an AAAA record with the link-local
IPv6 address), read once at startup.
The name is handled exactly like a reflected device that has no services: it
is announced, re-announced periodically, answers queries, and is withdrawn
with goodbye packets on
.BR SIGTERM .
No conflict detection is done: the name is assumed to be yours alone.
.SS Reflection
Within a segment, every interface announces the devices discovered on every
other interface of that segment.
Only devices that announced a hostname and at least one service are reflected;
chatty clients such as phones are tracked but not reflected.
Reflection is never transitive: a device reflected onto an interface is not
reflected again.
.PP
Reflected devices are announced with their original address, so the firewall
must allow the actual connections between the networks; mDNS only makes the
device discoverable.
.PP
The daemon answers queries for reflected devices, including known-answer
suppression, keeps them alive with periodic re-announcements, and withdraws
them with goodbye packets when the source device disappears.
If two devices would clash on hostname or address on a target interface, the
one from the interface listed first in the segment wins.
.SS Control socket
The daemon serves a local control socket, described under
.B [Ipc]
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
which
.BR mdnsctl (1)
uses to list segments and devices.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I config-file
Read the configuration from this file instead of
.IR /etc/mdnsd.conf .
The configuration is read once at startup; restart the daemon to apply changes.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Send goodbye packets for every reflected device, remove the control socket and
exit with status 0, so that clients drop the reflected devices immediately.
.PP
If the daemon is killed with
.B SIGKILL
or crashes, no goodbyes are sent, and clients keep the reflected devices until
their records expire, up to 75 minutes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped by
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT .
.TP
.B 1
Startup failed: the configuration is invalid, an interface cannot be opened, or
the control socket cannot be created.
The reason is printed on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock
Default control socket.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/mdnsd.service
systemd unit.
It runs the daemon as the unprivileged
.B mdnsd
user, starts it only once
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
exists, and restarts it every five seconds if it exits, which covers interfaces
that appear after boot.
.SH NOTES
Binding a socket to a single interface needs no privilege on Linux 5.7 and
newer.
.PP
The daemon binds with
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
so it can run next to Avahi while migrating, but do not run two reflectors
(this one and Avahi's) over the same interfaces.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run in the foreground with a configuration file of your own:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdns ./mdnsd.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run as a service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# cp /usr/share/doc/mdns/examples/mdnsd.conf /etc/mdnsd.conf
# $EDITOR /etc/mdnsd.conf
# systemctl enable \-\-now mdnsd.service
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
.BR mdnsctl (1)
